\DOC LOCAL_INV_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_INV_TAC : int -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances a machine-state symbolic-execution goal of the form
{eventually ... s_k} through one called subroutine, replacing the current state
variable by a fresh successor state and rewriting the state-dependent
assumptions accordingly.

\DESCRIBE
The single {int} argument is the index of the new state variable to introduce:
both the subject of the {eventually} conclusion and the state component of the
rewritten assumptions are renamed to that numbered state. The conclusion
changes only in its trailing state argument — the old state variable becomes
the new indexed one — while the postcondition body is untouched. All
assumptions mentioning the old state are discharged and replaced by
corresponding assumptions about the new state: the program-image assumption,
the register and memory-content equalities that persist, a new equality giving
the result of the subroutine call at its destination buffer, updated
program-counter and stack-pointer readings, and the frame/{MAYCHANGE}
assumption, reattached to the new state with an extra component added to it.
Purely mathematical and non-overlapping assumptions are kept unchanged and stay
in position, whereas the state-dependent ones are re-added at the end, so the
assumption list is reordered.
{
       A, z1(s_old) ... zp(s_old) ?- eventually ... s_old
       ============================================  LOCAL_INV_TAC n
       A, z1'(s_n) ... zq'(s_n) ?- eventually ... s_n
}

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single kind of transformation, on goals
whose conclusion is an {eventually} statement with a rich set of state
assumptions already present.

\ENDDOC
